Today's session focused on solving two classic Greedy and Interval problems from LeetCode.
Problem 1 :Leetcode 435. Non-overlapping Intervals
Approach:
 Sorted all intervals based on their ending time. Starting with the first interval, I greedily kept every interval whose start time was greater than or equal to the end of the previously selected interval. Whenever an overlap occurred, I removed the interval that ended later by simply keeping the one with the smaller end time. This greedy choice maximizes the number of non-overlapping intervals, giving the minimum removals.

Time Complexity: O(n log n) (Sorting)
 Space Complexity: O(1) (Ignoring sorting space)

Problem 2: 57. Insert Interval
Approach:
 Traversed the intervals in three phases:
Added all intervals that end before the new interval begins.
Merged all overlapping intervals with the new interval by updating its start and end.
Added the merged interval, followed by the remaining non-overlapping intervals.
This ensures the intervals remain sorted and merged in a single traversal after insertion.

Time Complexity: O(n)
 Space Complexity: O(n) (For the output list)

Key Takeaways
1) Learned how sorting by ending time leads to optimal solutions in interval scheduling problems.
2) Strengthened my understanding of interval merging and handling overlapping ranges efficiently.
3) Reinforced the power of greedy algorithms in producing optimal solutions with simple logic.
